    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for the info. the interlocking pattern makes sense, but we've been having some issues with the loads feeling \"off\" once they reach the upper levels. even when they look straight, it feels like something is unsettled or like the weight shifts unexpectedly during transit. is there a specific way to calculate if the center of gravity is too high, or is it more about how the individual items are placed within the pallet itself?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To calculate if your center of gravity is unstable, measure the load's weight edge-to-edge to ensure the center of gravity is located well within the base of support, not just at the geometric middle of the stack height. Uneven placement of individual items or heavy components on a single layer can create internal stress points that feel like shifting even when the pallet surface appears level. Regularly inspecting for internal voids or uneven weight distribution before transit is often more effective than relying solely on the external stacking pattern for stability.",
      "source": "target",
